# THE SHIPPED BACKFILL — the two admissions this station's own run records established.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (req-P0-A-NEW-USER-CANNOT-MINE-THE-PERMISS). The station's export withholds
# the store (~446 MB, not redistributable) and the STORE IS THE UNIT OF HISTORY, so a clone ships
# INTENT (sources.yml: enabled: true) and NO HISTORY (no diary). The permission test then refuses
# every source with "the diary holds no 'admitted' event" — true, and useless to a stranger, because
# there is no diary to hold anything. The rule is right; the bootstrap was missing. This is it.
#
# WHAT IT IS: FACTS, not lines. One JSON object per line, '#' comments allowed, no seq and no
# prev_event_sha256 — the chain belongs to the ONE writer (symbiont/diary.py::append), so a file in
# the package can never ship a link it cannot prove. `bootstrap_from_seed()` is the only thing that
# turns these into diary lines, and it only ever writes admitted{origin: backfill}, only into a
# store that holds NO history at all, and only for a source this sources.yml declares and enables.
# The line it writes carries the why `shipped seed — no admission history in this clone`, so the
# ledger is never silent about how a clone started.
#
# WHAT EACH LINE NAMES: the ARTEFACT the admission came from — the committed RUN RECORD that
# verified that release and its count (symbiont/fixtures/store/runs/arc-1.json, which ships and is
# pinned here by sha256) and the decision record that chose the fetch route (reports/adapters/…,
# which ships). The full-scale farm run records (78,348/78,348 and 77,645/77,645 rows) live in the
# live store and are NOT redistributed; each line says so rather than implying it carries them.
#
# A seed line is not a permission: it restores a decision a run record already documents. A source
# that was never admitted stays unadmitted, and a store that has a diary but lacks the admission
# still answers REFUSED[not-admitted] — this file is read ONLY when there is no diary at all.
